"use strict";

/* generated from story/scenes/act3_ch18.md — do not edit */

SC["a3c18_boat"]={img:"silk",
 t:()=>SYS(`CHAPTER EIGHTEEN — SILK ON THE WATER`)+P(`The boat finds you. That's the part you'll remember: you had the address, the network map, six weeks of planning, and the lacquered riverboat simply poles up to the Inn of the Patient Heron's dock at teatime, white as a bone, silent as snowfall, and a steward in grey presents a card that says, in needle-fine characters: <i>MADAM XIAN, OF THE NINTH REEL, REQUESTS THE PLEASURE.</i>`)+P(`Madam Xian is a courteous woman of middle years with mulberry-scented sleeves and the restful face of someone who has never once been argued out of anything. She pours the tea herself. It is your preferred tea, steeped the way you take it, which no one on this river knows.`)+P(`“The Knotless,” she says, warmly. “Or: the gold-haired servant of the Azure Cloud, tournament victor, auditor of inconvenient ledgers. The Court has read every story with gold thread in it — we bought them all, you know — and the remarkable thing is how little the stories exaggerate.” She sets down the pot. “Elder Kang was sloppy and is not mourned. Clearwater was gentle and is. You have questions. I am, genuinely, here to answer them. The Court does not lie, weaver. We have never once needed to.”`)+P(`And she doesn't. For an hour, over white tea on white water, she lays out the Court's case with the calm of arithmetic: the Weave of Heaven frays where foreign souls strike it (you have SEEN this, she observes, in a marsh, recently; was it not so?); the fraying spreads; left alone it drops skies on ten thousand somebodies. The furnaces are not appetite. They are <i>repair</i>: mending-heat for the Grand Loom that holds the fraying shut over half a continent. “Your existence is a wound, golden child,” she says, with what is unmistakably compassion. “That is not a moral judgment. Wounds are no one's fault. But they must be either mended, or put to work mending.”`)+((G.f.a3_feral=="calmed")?(P(`“The one in the marsh,” you say, and her face does its first real thing: a small, unfeigned sorrow. “Consignment forty-one. She tore loose half-wound; the fray never closed. Everything she suffers, the Weave suffers at scale, everywhere your kind lands. You CALMED her, the river says.” A measuring pause, the gentlest one yet. “The Court has been trying for forty years. You begin to see why we prefer the rare ones alive.”`)):(""))+P(`And then, mildly, setting down her cup, the sentence she came to say:`)+P(`“The consignment your blind friend mourns sits at the Grand Loom, half-woven and quite content. Wen Suyin asks after the mountain, some years. She would be glad, I think, of news.”`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Watch her threads while she talks — every weaver runs OUTWARD from the dantian, and you need to know what she runs on.`,req:()=>(G.insight>=12),rq:"(needs Insight 12)",do:()=>{G.f.a3_believer=true;addIns(1);note("Her threads run outward — but not to her limbs. Past them. Hundreds of filaments, finer than spider-silk, out through the hull and down the river toward the lake country, PAYING IN. She is not fed by the Loom like Kang was. She feeds it. Willingly. Continuously. Whatever the Court is, its officers are not parasites — they are believers, tithing themselves. That is enormously worse.");},go:"a3c18_pivot"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Keep your eyes on her face and your threads coiled. Some hours are for listening.`,do:()=>{G.heart++;note("+1 Heart. You listen the way Granny Sho taught you to sweep: completely. By the hour's end you could recite the Court's case in its own cadence — which, you suspect, is the only way you'll ever take it apart.");},go:"a3c18_pivot"}]};

SC["a3c18_pivot"]={t:()=>`<div class="divider">⁂</div>`+((G.sho>=3)?(P(`The teahouse goes quiet a heartbeat before the dock-boards speak. Tap. Tap. Tap.`)+P(`Granny Sho walks onto the white boat's deck like forty years of winter coming down a mountain, broom traded for the cane, blind eyes fixed on Madam Xian with an accuracy that makes the steward step back. She left the Azure Cloud the week your letter named the name; she has been working down the same river ever since, asking after mulberry-scented boats; and no one — you watch the whole deck fail to do it — no one moves to stop her.`)+P(`“Forty years,” she says, conversationally, to the most dangerous woman on the river. “I swept a thousand steps for forty years, listening for my sister under a mountain. And she was HERE. Downriver. Asking after the mountain, some years.” The cane comes down once, and the boat's lacquer cracks, hairline, stem to stern. “You will take me to her, silk-woman. Or you will explain to your Loom why its mending-heat could not mend THIS.”`)+P(`Madam Xian regards the blind sweeper for a long, genuinely interested moment — and then does the most frightening thing you have seen her do. She bows. “The Court remembers the sister,” she says. “Resonant, the recruiters wrote. The seed of a weaver, unsprouted. The invitation, Granny, has been open for forty years. It was never the Court that didn't send for you.” A glance at you, mild as milk. “It was the mountain that didn't ask.”`)):(P(`You think of Granny Sho, sweeping the bottom of a thousand steps, who never told you the name she lost — and who therefore doesn't know, and isn't here, and the letter you'll have to write tonight is suddenly the heaviest thing on this boat.`)))+P(`Madam Xian rises, the hour concluded, and extends the Court's actual business like a sweet at the end of a bitter dose: an invitation, needle-written on white silk. The Grand Loom. As <i>guest</i> — the word given weight, given warranty. “The rare ones are invited,” she says. “You would be the first in a generation to accept rather than be collected. The Lady of the Reels would so prefer it. Autumn, weaver. The boat will call again after the inspection.”`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`The silk is in your hand, and the choice is the act's whole question.`,go:"a3c18_choice"}]};

SC["a3c18_choice"]={t:()=>P(`The white boat waits. Three answers, and you've been turning them over since the tea went cold:`),
 c:()=>[{id:"c0",l:`Accept. Walk into the Court as a guest — the net is politeness, so hold the politeness to its warranty.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_court="guest";note("Madam Xian's pleasure is unfeigned and somehow that is the worst omen yet. “Autumn, then. Bring your appetite for wonders.” The white boat pours itself back into the river like silk through a ring.");},go:"a3c19_plan"},
  {id:"c1",l:`Refuse — with full courtesy, tea-bow and all. Not yet. Not on the Court's schedule.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_court="refused";note("She accepts the refusal like a move in a long game she's enjoying. “The invitation does not expire, weaver. The Court is PATIENT — it holds up half a sky; patience is the whole of its character.” The boat leaves. You are certain, without evidence, that something of hers stayed.");},go:"a3c19_plan"},
  {id:"c2",l:`Defy her. Tell the white boat what you told a furnace-keeper: it's going to hurt one of us.`,do:()=>{G.f.a3_court="defied";note("Madam Xian hears the threat the way a glacier hears weather. “Kang said something similar in his youth — it's in his file. The Court does not punish candor.” She pauses at the rail, and for one instant the mulberry sweetness opens onto something vast and patient and load-bearing. “But do remember, golden child: we are not the net. We are the people MENDING the net you are tearing simply by breathing. One of us is already hurt.” The boat leaves you standing in the wreckage of your own line.");},go:"a3c19_plan"}]};